# One known disagreement per line: `<id> | <shape> | <reason>`. The shape is the oracle's own label
# (printed with each DISAGREEMENT). The id names a fixed witness program in
# formal/SproutCore/Witnesses.lean; an entry whose witness no longer disagrees with its shape
# fails the run, so the list cannot rot, and an entry with no witness is an error
# (docs/lean-model-v0.md §6). Lines starting with `#` are comments.
#
# Compiler gap: spec §5.2.1 and §5.2.2 make an effectful right-hand side of `let ... in` and of a
# `do` `let` an error; the checker accepts it in an `!{IO}` function. Known and scheduled:
# docs/try-propagate-v0.md §4.5 and §9 Q15 (step 5 enforces it). The model follows the spec.
let-rhs-io | model-rejects/compiler-accepts: let-rhs-io | compiler does not enforce the pure right-hand side of let..in and do-let (spec §5.2.1, §5.2.1a)
# Spec gap: the compiler applies a syntactic value restriction to a top-level `let` (`is_syntactic_value`
# in infer.sprout, docs/fundamentals-code-review-handoff-2026-07-03.md W3) and rejects a non-value
# initialiser whose type still has free variables. spec §5.2 and §7 rule 16 say nothing about it.
value-restriction | model-accepts/compiler-rejects: value restriction | the spec does not state the value restriction on top-level let (compiler rejects non-value let with a polymorphic type)
# Spec gap: a top-level `let` that calls a pure function value bound by an earlier non-value
# top-level `let` is rejected as "performs IO when its module loads". The compiler gives a lambda's
# arrow a fresh effect variable; `f` is not generalised, so it stays open, and spec §6's note says
# "An unresolved effect variable counts as not pure". The spec never gives a lambda an effect
# variable (§5.3), so read literally the `let` is pure, which the model follows.
# `let f = let v = 0 in \(x: Bool) -> x` then `let g = f(false)`.
nongen-call | model-accepts/compiler-rejects: top-level _ performs IO when its module loads, but top-level bindings must be pure | the spec does not say whether an inferred lambda's arrow has an effect variable, which decides whether §6's unresolved-variable rule applies
# Compiler bug: passing a pure function value bound by a non-generalised top-level `let` (so its
# effect variable stays open) to an `!{IO}` slot, then calling it, makes the caller "perform IO".
# `effect_slot_meet` in unifier.sprout records the slot as a ceiling, but `bind_open` also binds
# the open variable to IO, and the variable is shared with every other use of the value. Spec §7
# rule 8: a ceiling from a written slot is a bound, and property 3: an unresolved variable is
# accepted. `let l = let v = false in \(x: Int) -> v`, `fn use(p: Int -> Bool !{IO})`, then
# `fn both() = if use(l) then l(0) else false`. The shape also covers any other "performs IO but
# is declared pure" the model does not see, so a model bug in IO tracking would hide here too.
nongen-slot | model-accepts/compiler-rejects: _ performs IO but is declared pure | compiler binds an open effect variable to IO from a slot ceiling, so a shared non-generalised function value reads back as IO (spec §7 rule 8, property 3)
# Compiler bug: a peer join (`if`) judges the verdict by branch order once a branch is itself a
# join. `if c then (if c then L1 else L2) else p` is rejected for an `!{IO}` function `p` and a
# slot that permits IO, while `if c then p else (if c then L1 else L2)` is accepted. Spec §7 rule
# 8: "A peer join is covered too ... the verdict does not depend on which branch is written
# first", and the note under rule 8 (property 2): branch order does not decide it. `if` joins
# through `infer_if_join` and `unify_actual_expected` in infer.sprout/unifier.sprout.
join-order | model-accepts/compiler-rejects: If branches must have same type: IO function where a pure one is required | compiler rejects a join of a nested join with an IO function, where the swapped order is accepted (spec §7 rule 8: branch order does not decide)
# Spec gap: the compiler rejects a branch after both `Bool` literals, after `()`, and after a
# tuple pattern of catch-alls as unreachable (`prefix_covers_toplevel` in infer.sprout). Spec §5.5's
# four cases name catch-alls, repeated literals and ADT constructors only; the model follows them.
unreachable-bool | model-accepts/compiler-rejects: Unreachable match branch | the spec does not say whether Bool counts as an ADT for §5.5's "after all constructors" case
unreachable-unit | model-accepts/compiler-rejects: Unreachable match branch | the spec does not say whether Unit counts as an ADT for §5.5's "after all constructors" case
unreachable-tuple | model-accepts/compiler-rejects: Unreachable match branch | the spec does not say whether a tuple counts as an ADT for §5.5's "after all constructors" case
# Compiler bug: `prefix_has_full_ctor` in infer.sprout counts an earlier branch as covering a
# constructor only when every sub-pattern is `_` or a variable. `C ()` or `C (a, b)` covers `C`
# completely too, so spec §5.5 makes a later `C _`, or a `_` after every constructor, unreachable.
unreachable-ctor-repeat | model-rejects/compiler-accepts: unreachable | compiler misses a repeat of a constructor an earlier branch covers without catch-alls (spec §5.5)
unreachable-after-ctors | model-rejects/compiler-accepts: unreachable | compiler misses a branch after every constructor is covered without catch-alls (spec §5.5)
# Compiler bug: spec §5.6 allows a lowercase type variable in a field only when the declaration's
# parameter list binds it; the compiler accepts `type T = | C a` and reads `a` as an opaque type.
free-tyvar | model-rejects/compiler-accepts: unknown-type | compiler accepts a field type variable the declaration does not bind (spec §5.6)
# Spec gap: §5.6 checks the type names in a field, not how many arguments each is applied to. The
# compiler accepts `type T = | C (P Int Int)` for a one-parameter `P`, and no value of `C` can then
# be built or matched. The model rejects it: such a field has no type.
field-type-arity | model-rejects/compiler-accepts: type-arity | the spec does not say that a field's type must apply each type to its parameter count
# Compiler bug, the `join-order` one through `match`: arms join as peers (spec §7 rule 5, rule 8),
# but `| 0 -> \x -> x | 1 -> \y -> y | _ -> p` with `p: Int -> Int !{IO}` against an `!{IO}`
# result is rejected, while the same arms with `p` first are accepted.
join-order-match | model-accepts/compiler-rejects: Match branch type mismatch: IO function where a pure one is required | compiler rejects a match whose arms join two pure lambdas and then an IO function, where `p` first is accepted (spec §7 rule 8: branch order does not decide)
